1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:10,153
Imagine a robot in a narrow corridor, beside a workshop with several tools. In the corridor, its skills mostly move it forward or backward.

2
00:00:10,573 --> 00:00:20,503
Inside the workshop, those skills could produce many more distinct outcomes. Before using a tool, the robot has to reach a place where tools are available.

3
00:00:21,253 --> 00:00:33,177
That creates two learning problems. One policy practices distinguishable behaviors from a starting state. Another policy learns to reach promising starting states in the first place.

4
00:00:33,597 --> 00:00:43,731
Scaling Empowered Agents builds around this separation. We will call these the skill policy and the preparation policy, and keep their jobs visually distinct.

5
00:00:44,481 --> 00:00:53,100
The skill policy asks what different effects it can produce here. The preparation policy asks where it should go to make those effects possible.

6
00:00:53,520 --> 00:01:03,567
Our workshop is a teaching example. The important feature is the dependency: preparing the world can expand the set of choices an agent will have later.

7
00:01:05,767 --> 00:01:15,122
At the workshop entrance, choose one of four skill labels. Run that skill, then show its future state to a classifier that tries to recover the label.

8
00:01:15,542 --> 00:01:26,424
If the resulting states are distinct, the classifier can identify the skill. Its log probability gain over the prior supplies an intrinsic reward, as in DIAYN.

9
00:01:27,174 --> 00:01:36,157
This time, also tell the classifier where the skill started. A future state can have different meanings depending on the starting state that came before it.

10
00:01:36,577 --> 00:01:46,827
For example, ending beside a tool says something different if you began beside it already. Conditioning on the start isolates the information carried by the skill.

11
00:01:47,577 --> 00:01:56,645
There are now two reasons for a low score. The skills may produce the same effects, or the classifier may fail to recognize effects that are different.

12
00:01:57,065 --> 00:02:06,985
Keep those failures separate. A low learned information estimate does not, by itself, prove that the starting state has few genuinely achievable options.

13
00:02:09,185 --> 00:02:18,390
Testing every skill from every state would be expensive. So learn a value function that predicts the future intrinsic return of the skill policy.

14
00:02:18,810 --> 00:02:28,091
Think of the value as a fast estimate of how readable the skill's future effects will be. It replaces repeated long experiments with a prediction.

15
00:02:28,841 --> 00:02:38,909
The paper uses a discounted future-state distribution. Its weights include a factor of one minus gamma, making the entire distribution sum to one.

16
00:02:39,329 --> 00:02:50,480
An ordinary discounted reward sum does not include that normalization. Keep the scale factor when connecting a value estimate to a normalized information quantity.

17
00:02:51,230 --> 00:03:04,008
With exact expectations and the appropriate decoder, the population variational objective is a lower bound on information. A fitted neural value is an approximation to that objective.

18
00:03:04,428 --> 00:03:13,529
Its prediction errors can go either way. A high number on a learned critic is therefore not a mathematical certificate of equally high empowerment.

19
00:03:15,729 --> 00:03:25,744
Now let the preparation policy act. It moves from the corridor toward states where the learned skill value is high. In our example, it enters the workshop.

20
00:03:26,164 --> 00:03:35,775
Only then do we select a skill and demonstrate an effect. The orange preparation path and the colored skill paths belong to different levels of the system.

21
00:03:36,525 --> 00:03:46,753
As skills improve, the workshop's estimated usefulness can increase. Better preparation then brings the agent to states that support richer skill practice.

22
00:03:47,172 --> 00:03:57,860
This feedback can help learning, but it can also amplify errors. A bad value estimate may attract the preparation policy to a location that only looks promising.

23
00:03:58,610 --> 00:04:10,252
So we should occasionally return to direct evidence. Fix a starting state, sample skills, and inspect the actual resulting distributions rather than only the predicted value.

24
00:04:10,672 --> 00:04:21,134
The key comparison is between opportunities that exist, opportunities the current skills realize, and opportunities the current measurement system manages to recognize.

25
00:04:23,334 --> 00:04:33,126
Suppose two tools produce visibly different physical outcomes, but our observation encoder maps both outcomes to exactly the same feature vector.

26
00:04:33,546 --> 00:04:43,766
The classifier cannot distinguish them through that representation. Information about the chosen skill has been lost before the decoder even gets to see the data.

27
00:04:44,516 --> 00:04:55,235
Now add a flashing background light that changes independently of the skill. The observation becomes more variable, but the light carries no information about our choice.

28
00:04:55,655 --> 00:05:08,390
In the ideal information calculation, independent nuisance variation cancels. A finite learned system can still waste capacity modeling it, which makes representation design matter.

29
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:20,169
Three bottlenecks now stand between real options and the reported score: the skills themselves, the representation of their outcomes, and the learned decoder or value.

30
00:05:20,589 --> 00:05:31,266
Improving one bottleneck need not fix the others. This is why a larger reported intrinsic return is not enough evidence that an agent became more capable.

31
00:05:33,466 --> 00:05:44,569
To evaluate the system, hold the starting state fixed and test the skill family. Do different labels reliably produce different outcomes, including on held-out experience?

32
00:05:44,989 --> 00:05:55,688
Then evaluate the preparation policy separately. Does it reach states where those distinctions become easier to achieve, or merely states where the critic is overconfident?

33
00:05:56,438 --> 00:06:09,152
The paper develops practical neural approximations to make this learning scalable, including approximations to the relevant classifier structure. Our boxes are the conceptual decomposition.

34
00:06:09,572 --> 00:06:21,145
They are not a complete implementation or a claim that every approximation is exact. The companion gives the equations, source references, and a small inspectable experiment.

35
00:06:21,895 --> 00:06:28,510
Keep the workshop picture: first learn what you can do from a place, then learn to reach places where you can do more.

36
00:06:28,930 --> 00:06:40,236
The value function makes that second search practical. Its usefulness depends on how well the skills, observations, and decoder measure the control the world actually permits.
